<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-column-null-space" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Column Space, Null Space, and Rank</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every matrix carries two subspaces with it: the span of its columns, and the set of
      vectors it sends to zero. Their dimensions are linked by a single identity, and
      together they let us restate the Invertible Matrix Theorem in geometric language.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-column-space">
    <title>Column Space and Rank</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-column-space">
      <title>Column Space and Rank</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The <term>column space</term> of <m>A</m> is the span of the columns of
                <m>A</m> and is denoted by <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The <term>rank</term> of <m>A</m> is the dimension of the column space of
                <m>A</m> and is denoted by <m>\operatorname{rank} A</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-rank-column-space">
      <title>Finding the Rank and Column Space</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the rank of the matrix <m>A</m> and describe the column space efficiently:
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\
              1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
              3 \amp 7 \amp 8 \amp 6 \amp 6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let us find the reduced row-echelon form of <m>A</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                3 \amp 7 \amp 8 \amp 6 \amp 6
              \end{bmatrix}
              \amp \xrightarrow[-3r_1 + r_3]{-r_1 + r_2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}
              \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp -9 \amp 9 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The pivots sit in columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>. Note that
          <md>
            \operatorname{Col} A = \operatorname{Span}\left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
            \qquad \text{and hence} \qquad
            \operatorname{rank} A = 2.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Why does this work? Writing <m>\vec{R}_1, \ldots, \vec{R}_5</m> for the columns
          of the reduced row-echelon matrix, note that <m>\{ \vec{R}_1, \vec{R}_2 \}</m>
          is a linearly independent set and
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{R}_1, \ldots, \vec{R}_5 \} = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{R}_1, \vec{R}_2 \}</m>.
          Also <m>x_1\vec{R}_1 + x_2\vec{R}_2 = \vec{0}</m> only has the trivial solution.
          Row operations do not change which linear relations hold among the columns, so
          similar statements hold for the columns of <m>A</m>:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{A}_1, \ldots, \vec{A}_5 \}
            = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{A}_1, \vec{A}_2 \},
          </md>
          and <m>x_1\vec{A}_1 + x_2\vec{A}_2 = \vec{0}</m> only has the trivial solution.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-columns-interactive">
          <caption>
            All five columns of <m>A</m> drawn as vectors in <m>\R^3</m>, labelled
            <m>a_1, \ldots, a_5</m>. The pivot columns <m>\vec{A}_1 = (1,1,3)</m> and
            <m>\vec{A}_2 = (2,3,7)</m> are highlighted in olive green; the other three
            columns are grey, and every one of them lies in the olive green plane
            <m>\operatorname{Span}\{\vec{A}_1, \vec{A}_2\} = \operatorname{Col} A</m>.
            Five columns, but only two independent directions:
            <m>\operatorname{rank} A = 2</m>. Press <em>animate</em> to watch the
            columns appear one at a time, and drag to rotate <mdash /> viewed edge-on,
            the plane is razor thin.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-columns" iframe="demos-manim/columns.html?mat=1,2,1,3,2:1,3,6,0,2:3,7,8,6,6&amp;range=9" width="66%" aspect="5:6"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <observation xml:id="obs-rank-is-pivots">
      <p>
        The rank of <m>A</m> is equal to the number of pivots in <m>A</m>. A basis for
        <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m> is given by the columns of the <em>original</em>
        matrix <m>A</m> in the pivot positions.
      </p>
    </observation>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-null-space">
    <title>Null Space of a Matrix and Kernel of a Transformation</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-null-space">
      <title>Null Space of a Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix. The <term>null space</term> of
          <m>A</m> is the set of all solutions of the homogeneous equation
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} A
            = \left\{ \vec{x} \in \R^n \; : \; A\vec{x} = \vec{0} \right\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-null-space">
      <title>Finding a Null Space</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\operatorname{Nul} A</m> for
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We solve <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
              \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-r_2]{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          With <m>z = t</m> free, the rows give <m>y = t</m> and <m>x = -3t</m>, so
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = t \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad
            \operatorname{Nul} A = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
          </md>
          which is a line through the origin.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also, since the spanning set of <m>\operatorname{Nul} A</m> consists of a single
          vector, we have <m>\dim \operatorname{Nul} A = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-null-column-interactive">
          <caption>
            Both subspaces of this matrix at once, for <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> with
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The deep wine line on the left is
            <m>\operatorname{Nul} A = \operatorname{Span}\{(-3,1,1)\}</m>: drag
            <m>\vec{x}</m> along it and the output never leaves <m>\vec{0}</m>. The olive green
            plane on the right is <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m>: drag <m>\vec{b}</m> and
            only outputs on that plane are reachable. A line plus a plane makes
            <m>1 + 2 = 3</m> dimensions <mdash /> the rank<ndash />nullity relation of
            the next subsection. (Drag inside a pane to rotate it.)
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-null-column" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,2,1:0,-1,1:2,3,3&amp;x=0,0,0&amp;show=true&amp;lock=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The null space belongs to a matrix. The same idea for a transformation has its own
      name.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-kernel">
      <title>Kernel of a Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation. The
          <term>kernel</term> of <m>T</m> is the set of all inputs that <m>T</m> sends to
          the zero vector:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Ker} T
            = \left\{ \vec{x} \in \R^n \; : \; T(\vec{x}) = \vec{0} \right\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <observation xml:id="obs-kernel-is-null-space">
      <title>Two Names, One Set</title>
      <p>
        For a matrix transformation <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, the condition
        <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m> is the equation <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
        Therefore
        <md>
          \operatorname{Ker} T_A = \operatorname{Nul} A.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        In these notes the two words are kept apart: <m>\operatorname{Nul}</m> is
        always followed by a matrix, and <m>\operatorname{Ker}</m> by a transformation.
        We speak of the <em>null space of the matrix</em> <m>A</m> and of the
        <em>kernel of the transformation</em> <m>T_A</m>. Both live in the domain
        <m>\R^n</m>, not in the codomain <m>\R^m</m>.
      </p>
    </observation>

    <example xml:id="eg-null-space-and-kernel">
      <title>The Null Space of <m>A</m> and the Kernel of <m>T_A</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 4 \\ -1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          and let <m>T_A : \R^2 \to \R^3</m> be its matrix transformation,
          <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p>Find <m>\operatorname{Nul} A</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find <m>\operatorname{Ker} T_A</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find all vectors <m>\vec{x} \in \R^2</m> that <m>T_A</m> sends to the
                same output as <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> We solve <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
              2 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
              -1 \amp -2 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow[r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          With <m>y = t</m> free, the first row gives <m>x = -2t</m>, so
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} A
            = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
          </md>
          a line through the origin in <m>\R^2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> By <xref ref="def-kernel" text="type-global" />, the kernel of
          <m>T_A</m> consists of the vectors <m>\vec{x} \in \R^2</m> with
          <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m>, that is, with <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>. This
          is the equation solved in part (a), so
          <md>
            \operatorname{Ker} T_A = \operatorname{Nul} A
            = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
          </md>
          Parts (a) and (b) describe one set from two points of view: as the solutions of
          a homogeneous system whose coefficient matrix is <m>A</m>, and as the inputs
          that <m>T_A</m> sends to the zero vector. As a check,
          <md>
            T_A \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -2 + 2 \\ -4 + 4 \\ 2 - 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Notice that the kernel is a line in the domain <m>\R^2</m>, although the
          outputs of <m>T_A</m> are vectors in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> First,
          <m>T_A \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Since <m>T_A</m> is linear,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T_A(\vec{x}) = T_A \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
              \amp \iff
              T_A \left( \vec{x} - \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right) = \vec{0}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \iff
              \vec{x} - \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \in \operatorname{Ker} T_A.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          So the vectors we want are
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            + t \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad t \in \R,
          </md>
          the line through <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> parallel to the
          kernel. For instance, <m>t = 1</m> gives
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and indeed
          <md>
            T_A \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -1 + 4 \\ -2 + 8 \\ 1 - 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          This is <xref ref="thm-particular-plus-homogeneous" text="type-global" /> in the
          language of transformations: two inputs have the same output exactly when
          they differ by a vector of the kernel. In particular <m>T_A</m> is not
          one-to-one, because its kernel contains nonzero vectors
          (<xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global" />).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      <em>Explore the kernel.</em> In
      <xref ref="fig-kernel-interactive" text="type-global" /> you move an input
      <m>\vec{x}</m> around the domain <m>\R^2</m> of the transformation <m>T_A</m> of
      <xref ref="eg-null-space-and-kernel" text="type-global" />. Before starting,
      predict what happens to the output when the input slides along a line parallel to
      the kernel.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-kernel-interactive">
      <caption>
        The kernel of <m>T_A</m> for
        <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 4 \\ -1 \amp -2 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
        The wine line is
        <m>\operatorname{Ker} T_A = \operatorname{Nul} A
        = \operatorname{Span}\{(-2,1)\}</m>. In the interactive figure the sliders, or
        the handle at the tip of the vector, set the input; the readout gives
        <m>T_A(\vec{x})</m>, which is the zero vector exactly when the input is on the
        wine line, and the dashed line marks all inputs with the same output. The
        printed picture shows that <m>(1,1)</m> and <m>(-1,2)</m>, which differ by the
        kernel vector <m>(-2,1)</m>, are both sent to <m>(3,6,-3)</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-kernel" iframe="demos-manim/kernel.html" width="100%" aspect="3:5">
        <shortdescription>The plane of inputs of a transformation from R 2 to R 3, with its kernel drawn as a line through the origin; sliders move an input vector, a readout gives its output, and a dashed line parallel to the kernel marks all inputs with the same output.</shortdescription>
        <static>
          <image source="images/ch5-kernel" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>The xy-plane with the kernel, the line through the origin and the point (-2, 1), and a dashed parallel line through the points (1, 1) and (-1, 2), which have the same output (3, 6, -3).</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-rank-nullity">
    <title>The Rank<ndash />Nullity Relation</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-rank-nullity">
      <title>Rank and Nullity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix. Then
          <md>
            \operatorname{rank}(A) + \dim \operatorname{Nul} A = n.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-rank-nullity">
      <title>Checking the Rank<ndash />Nullity Relation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          from <xref ref="eg-null-space" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          After row reduction, we saw that
          <md>
            A \; \longrightarrow \;
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which has two leading <m>1</m>s, hence <m>\operatorname{rank} A = 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also, we saw that <m>\dim \operatorname{Nul} A = 1</m>. Furthermore <m>A</m> is
          <m>3 \times 3</m>, which means <m>n = 3</m>. Then
          <md>
            \underbrace{\operatorname{rank}(A)}_{2} + \underbrace{\dim \operatorname{Nul} A}_{1}
            = \underbrace{n}_{3}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-rank-nullity-interactive">
          <caption>
            The rank<ndash />nullity relation seen for <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>. In
            the input pane, the deep wine line is <m>\operatorname{Nul} A</m>
            (dimension <m>1</m>): the olive green <m>\vec{x}</m> starts at the null-space
            vector <m>(-3,1,1)</m>, and the entire line is sent to <m>\vec{0}</m>. In
            the output pane, the olive green plane is <m>\operatorname{Col} A</m> (dimension
            <m>2</m>). Of the three input dimensions, one is crushed to zero and two
            survive as the column space: <m>1 + 2 = 3 = n</m>. Drag <m>\vec{x}</m> or
            <m>\vec{b}</m>, or drag inside a pane to rotate it.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-rank-nullity" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,2,1:0,-1,1:2,3,3&amp;x=-3,1,1&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-imt-continued">
    <title>The Invertible Matrix Theorem (Continued)</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-imt-continued">
      <title>The Invertible Matrix Theorem, Continued</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. The following conditions are
          equivalent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>A</m> is an invertible matrix.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\operatorname{Col} A = \R^n</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\operatorname{rank} A = n</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\operatorname{Nul} A = \{ \vec{0} \}</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" /> we discussed
          the following equivalences, which we can now express in terms of rank, null
          space, and column space of <m>A</m>:
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>A</m> is an invertible matrix <m>\iff</m> the columns of <m>A</m> are
                linearly independent <m>\iff</m> <m>\operatorname{rank} A = n</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>A</m> is an invertible matrix <m>\iff</m> the columns of <m>A</m> span
                <m>\R^n</m> <m>\iff</m> <m>\operatorname{Col} A = \R^n</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>A</m> is an invertible matrix <m>\iff</m> <m>A\vec{X} = \vec{0}</m> has
                only the trivial solution <m>\iff</m>
                <m>\operatorname{Nul} A = \{ \vec{0} \}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
  </subsection>
</section>
